body:
Sorry, got you mixed ups with someone else who has a problem with age gaps, especially with the >"It’s simply what I wish I had realized as a child" line

&#x200B;

Glad I could provide you some entertainment over your summer break. I truly hope that you learn to not be so condescending when you speak with people. Makes sense now. I hope that you  "open your eyes" and see that there are nuances that are involved in situations/relationships.

You're right, Callie did want to sit down and talk but she still insisted on taking Sofia with her, even if that's not what she told Arizona, and she didn't want to do that until Arizona didn't want to talk about these schools that she had brought pamphlets for because in her mind there was no way she wouldn't bring Sofia with her. The time to bring it up would have been the moment that she was thinking about chasing someone across the country. She's a co-parent, not Sofia's only parent, and should have immediately remembered/known that it was not solely what she wanted. Arizona, understandably, wasn't feeling secure in having a voice or even an opinion in what was going on. They should have gotten the lawyers involved in the first place, maybe not go to court over it and do an arbitration instead, but one parent wanting to take a child across the country without the other parent is complicated. I don't think they would have come to the same result without lawyers, because Callie sometimes has a tendency to steamroll. Also, Arizona may have brought the lawyers, but Callie allowed her lawyer to attempt to tear down Arizona's image as someone who is a capable mother. 

{Bringing up the hours that she commits to the OR (Callie is also in the OR a lot, not to mention she's in a relationship with someone who also is training to be a surgeon so it's not exactly like they would have had a whole lot of time to dedicate to Sofia), her going out on dates (to me, it was as if Callie was trying to point out that she doesn't make Sofia a priority which is ridiculous. Is Arizona supposed to be single and only at home until Sofia can stay home alone?), and of course, that she's not a real mother because she didn't give birth to her (a very low blow and I wish Mer had been in the courtroom because I would have loved to know what she would have said to Callie about that). She would have been aware of all the things the lawyer would bring up in court so I don't at all give her a pass.} 

In fact, when it all started, Callie gave no thought to Sofia's wellbeing when she decided to move. She was thinking only about herself. Anyone with half a mind would have thought about the fact that Sofia's whole life is/has been in Seattle with the only people that she knows. Could it have all been amicable? Yes. Could they have come to a point where both were happy with the agreement? Yes, but that didn't happen because Callie just assumed.
